 "rulebook": "IWF Technical and Competition Rules & Regulations (International Weightlifting Federation; quoted from memory of the 2020s editions): 2.3 the clean and jerk, 2.5 incorrect movements, 3.1 the platform, 3.3 the barbell",
 "deviations": [
  "3.3 (the barbell's load): the barbell is the lightest one the men's equipment gives on full-size discs: the 20.0 kg bar, one 10.0 kg disc of 0.45 m (the lightest disc of the full diameter) and one 2.5 kg collar on each sleeve, 45.0 kg; there is no choice of weight and no progression of attempts, because the task is one attempt",
  "3.3 (what the rule leaves open): the bar's 20.0 kg are split 7 kg for the shaft and 6.5 kg for each revolving sleeve, a disc is 0.034 m wide and a collar 0.07 m, and a disc keeps 0.4 of its speed when it bounces on the platform; these are estimates, the rule gives no numbers for them",
  "3.3 (the grip): the robot's hands have no fingers; a closed grip holds the bar's grip section rigidly to the palm when the bar's surface is within 3.5 cm of the palm point, so the hook grip, chalk and the strength of the fingers are not simulated",
  "2.3.1 (the clean): the referee judges the end of the clean, not the movement: the bar at the shoulders (its axis within 0.15 m of both shoulder joints; the robot's rigid chest stands 0.07 m ahead of them, so the bar cannot lie on the collarbones) with the legs extended, the feet on one line and everything motionless for 0.5 s (the rule says \"motionless\" and gives no time); that the bar came there in a single movement is not judged, because that needs a human reading of the pull",
  "the rules' \"at the shoulders\" and \"overhead\" assume a lifter who stands under the bar; so that neither position can be reached bent over or with the bar behind the back, in both the trunk must be upright (the cosine of its tilt at least 0.94), the bar's centre no more than 0.25 m (horizontally) from the point midway between the shoulder joints, and the bar within 20.0 degrees of the line through the shoulder joints (seen from above)",
  "2.5 (incorrect movements): judged here are the unfinished attempt above knee height, a part other than the feet on the platform, leaving the platform, the incomplete extension of the arms and legs at the finish and the feet not on one line; not judged, because they need a human judge's reading of the movement: the press-out and a pause while the arms extend, pulling from the hang, the bar touching the thighs, the elbows or upper arms touching the knees or thighs, an uneven extension of the arms, the oscillation of the bar before the jerk",
  "2.3.2 (the finish): \"fully extended\" is read from the robot's joints: each elbow within 10.0 degrees and each knee within 10.0 degrees of straight; \"motionless\" is the bar and the pelvis slower than 0.1 m/s; the three referees' \"down\" signal is given by the simulator when the final position has been held for 2.0 s, and the attempt ends there: lowering the bar after the signal is not part of the task",
  "6.6 (the clock): the athlete's one minute runs here from the start of the episode to the down signal (60 s for the whole attempt); the rule's minute runs from the call to the platform to the start of the lift, and there is no call here"
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Clean and jerk the barbell: lift it from the platform to your shoulders, then to locked arms overhead, and hold it there motionless until the down signal.",
  "rules": "The barbell is the IWF men's bar (20.0 kg, 2.2 m long; the grip section between the sleeves is 1.31 m long and 0.028 m thick; the sleeves are 0.415 m long and 0.05 m thick and revolve on the bar) with one 10.0 kg rubber disc of 0.45 m and one 2.5 kg collar on each sleeve: 45.0 kg. It lies on the competition platform (4.0 m by 4.0 m, 0.1 m high), on which you stand. A hand can grip the grip section anywhere along it. Lift-off: the bar may leave the platform only in both hands: you fail the moment the bar's axis is more than 0.03 m above where it lies at rest while fewer than two hands grip it. The attempt: once the bar's centre has been 0.48 m above the platform (knee height) the lift must be finished: from then on you fail if either hand lets go or if any part of the barbell touches the platform before the down signal. The clean: the bar must first rest at your shoulders: its axis within 0.15 m of both shoulder joints, both hands on it, both knees within 10.0 degrees of straight, both feet on the platform and on one line, the bar and your pelvis slower than 0.1 m/s, and you under the bar (your trunk upright: the cosine of its tilt at least 0.94; the bar's centre no more than 0.25 m, horizontally, from the point midway between your shoulder joints; the bar, seen from above, within 20.0 degrees of the line through them), with the attempt begun (the bar has been above knee height and touches nothing but you), all of it without a break for 0.5 s. You fail if the bar's axis is more than 0.25 m above the centre of your head before that (a lift from the platform to overhead in one movement is a snatch). The jerk and the finish: after the clean, bring the bar overhead. The final position is: the bar's axis at least 0.25 m above the centre of your head, both hands on it, both elbows within 10.0 degrees of straight (the angle between the upper arm's and the forearm's long axes), both knees within 10.0 degrees of straight (the angle hip, knee, ankle), both feet on the platform and on one line (their centres no more than 0.05 m apart along the horizontal direction square to the bar; under a bar tilted more than 60.0 degrees from level the feet never count as on one line), the bar and your pelvis slower than 0.1 m/s, and you under the bar as in the clean (trunk upright, the bar's centre over the middle of your shoulders, the bar along your shoulder line). Once it is held for 2.0 s without a break the down signal is given: that is success, and the attempt ends. At any time you fail if a part of you other than your feet touches the platform, if any part of you or of the barbell touches the floor beside the platform, or after 60 s. There is no other fall rule: you may squat as low as you like, and the bar may touch your body.",
  "venue": "You stand upright in the middle of the platform, facing +x, your soles 0.1 m above the floor, in the rest pose (upper arms down, forearms forward). The barbell lies on the platform across your front, parallel to your shoulders and centred on you: its axis is 0.1 m ahead of your pelvis and 0.225 m above the platform (above your feet, just clear of your shins), the discs 0.69 m to each side of its middle. Your shoulder joints are 1.46 m above the platform when you stand, 0.30 m apart; the centre of your head is at 1.70 m, its top at 1.79 m; your knee joints are at 0.48 m. In the `stand` action's stance your knees are bent by about 21 degrees, and your elbows are at a right angle in the rest pose: neither is extended. Your elbow joints have no stop at the straight arm (they bend both ways from it). The platform's edges are 2 m away in every direction.",
  "difficulty": "The barbell weighs two thirds of the robot: held at the shoulders it asks about twice the torque the elbow motors give (18 N m), so the arms cannot simply carry it there. Nothing balances the robot under a load, and no built-in action bends down to the bar or straightens the knees."
 },
